# computes InvertINElevation / InvertOUTElevation for a sanitary network (SanitaryMainLines) from MH rim elev (SanitaryManholes), and back-fills ManholeDepth

# rim elev is source of truth from DEM
# InvertIN, InvertOUT, and ManholeDepth all derived/adjusted for flow

# addition: MINIMUM-COVER CHECK against terrain samples along each pipe (from a "Generate Points Along Lines") for potench low point in the middle of the run

# 1. Push RimElevation from SanitaryManholes into
#   UpstreamMHRimElevation / DownstreamMHRimElevation on the pipes
# 2. Build a directed graph per Basin: pipe A -> pipe B when
#   pipe A's DownstreamMH = pipe B's UpstreamMH.
# 3. Topologically walk each basin's network (Kahn's algorithm), so a
#   pipe is only processed once every pipe feeding into it has already
#   been resolved (handles converging pipes)
# 4. Headwater pipes (no upstream pipe in the basin) seeded from
#   the upstream manhole: InvertIN = UpstreamMHRimElevation - ManholeDepth,
#   falling back to a default minimum cover depth if ManholeDepth is
#   null/zero.
# 5. For every pipe: InvertOUT = InvertIN - max(length * MIN_SLOPE, MIN_DROP_AT_JUNCTION)
# 6. MINIMUM-COVER CHECK: interpolate the pipe's own invert elevation at each point's
#   position (as a fraction of the pipe's length from the upstream end)
#   and compare it to (terrain elevation - MIN_COVER). If any sampled
#   point along the pipe would violate minimum cover BOTH InvertIN and InvertOUT for that pipe
#   are shifted down by the worst deficit found, preserving slope. Shift is applied before propagating InvertOUT downstream,
#   so it cascades correctly through the rest of the network.
# 7. Where pipes converge: next pipe's InvertIN = min(incoming InvertOUTs) - JUNCTION_LOSS
# 8. REMOVE----Final check: each basin's topological headwater(s) should sit at/near
#   that basin's known max elevation (BASIN_MAX_ELEVATION below). Large
#   deviation usually means UpstreamMH/DownstreamMH got reversed
#   somewhere in that basin, or the true headwater isn't connected.
# 9. ManholeDepth recalced for every MH as RimElevation - (lowest invert elevation of any pipe touching it),
#   and written back- depths change to make network work
# 10. InvertINElevation / InvertOUTElevation are written back to the pipes.

